WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.243
Tucked between the Alps, at the border of
Italy and Switzerland is a restaurant.

00:00:05.243 --> 00:00:09.154
For tourists visiting from around the world,
the Rifugio Guide del Cervino

00:00:09.154 --> 00:00:13.250
offers a break from winter sports for food,
drinks, lodging

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:17.542
and as this reviewer called it: 
“A great Italian experience.”

00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:19.856
But something unexpected is happening here.

00:00:19.856 --> 00:00:22.677
The nearby Theodul Glacier is melting.

00:00:22.677 --> 00:00:23.662
And as it does

00:00:23.662 --> 00:00:28.948
this little Italian ski lodge is on the verge
of moving into Switzerland.

00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:40.367
Most of Italy's land border follows the watershed
along the Alps.

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:41.792
There is this imaginary line 

00:00:41.792 --> 00:00:45.236
that divides the water that falls in the Mediterranean Sea in the south.

00:00:45.236 --> 00:00:48.115
Or in the north, into the Black Sea 
via the Dunabe.

00:00:48.115 --> 00:00:49.590
Or to the North Sea.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.843
That’s Marco Ferrari.

00:00:50.843 --> 00:00:53.890
He wrote a book about this border 
with architect Andrea Bagnato

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:57.233
and visual designer Elisa Pasqual.

00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:00.994
This watershed line traces many of the 
highest peaks of the Alps 

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:03.517
forming a natural border between countries.

00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:07.012
Much of it is marked by over 8,000
boundary stones like these.

00:01:07.012 --> 00:01:09.815
Some of which have been around 
since the 16th century.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.237
But in the most rugged terrain, where physical
markers are few and far between.

00:01:15.237 --> 00:01:19.170
Swiss, Italian, and Austrian cartographic
agencies have long maintained

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.619
official border records with 
meticulous measurements

00:01:21.619 --> 00:01:23.882
and annotated pictures like these.

00:01:23.882 --> 00:01:26.170
Some of these areas are on top of glaciers.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.340
Their surfaces are too reflective for satellites

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.459
which are often used to measure borders.

00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:33.125
The work by the surveyors is done manually.

00:01:33.125 --> 00:01:37.456
So every few years, there's 
a commission of surveyors

00:01:37.456 --> 00:01:40.440
composed of members of both countries

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.490
that actually walk the border and
look how it has changed.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.819
As early as the 1920s, surveyors noticed a
“slow but progressive” shrinking

00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:52.255
among glaciers, and that “some had disappeared".

00:01:52.255 --> 00:01:57.295
But by the 1990s, it became clear that summer
melt had outpaced winter accumulation.

00:01:57.295 --> 00:02:02.009
The surveyors realized that the glaciers were
melting and not accumulating ice anymore.

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:07.927
This kind of cyclical change in the shape
and geometry of the watershed became 

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.245
more and more permanent.

00:02:10.245 --> 00:02:13.140
And as glaciers shifted so did the watershed

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.640
and so did the border.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.156
Here’s what that looks like.

00:02:17.156 --> 00:02:21.090
On many Alpine peaks, a glacier ridge forms
the watershed boundary line

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.146
and thus the national border.

00:02:23.146 --> 00:02:26.360
But as those glaciers melt, their highest
point might shift

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.282
often dozens of meters away.

00:02:28.282 --> 00:02:31.519
If they melt far enough, they even reveal
underlying rock peaks

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:33.828
which then become the border line.

00:02:33.828 --> 00:02:38.587
These black X’s mark the old national boundary
between Italy and Switzerland.

00:02:38.587 --> 00:02:42.659
But if you trace today’s national boundary
and compare it to old maps

00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:46.206
you can see all the places where 
the boundary line has moved.

00:02:46.206 --> 00:02:48.239
In the most extreme case yet in the Alps

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:52.382
shifts in the Theodul Glacier moved the border
150 meters.

00:02:52.382 --> 00:02:56.131
That put this Italian ski lift on Swiss soil.

00:02:56.131 --> 00:03:00.775
And the Rifugio Guide del Cervino 
might be next.

00:03:00.775 --> 00:03:04.790
Typically when countries renegotiate borders,
there’s a careful process to make sure

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.247
that neither country gains territory 
at the other’s expense.

00:03:08.247 --> 00:03:11.200
But recently, Italy signed a new type of agreement.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.609
First with Austria in 2006, and then with
Switzerland in 2009.

00:03:15.609 --> 00:03:18.239
That recognized the Alps as a “mobile border".

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:21.674
Acknowledging that the border was subject
to changes in the natural world 

00:03:21.674 --> 00:03:23.469
outside of those countries’ control.

00:03:24.893 --> 00:03:27.935
Fortunately, the Rifugio is 
the only inhabited place

00:03:27.935 --> 00:03:29.662
along this shifting border.

00:03:29.662 --> 00:03:33.939
And if it becomes Swiss, it’ll be subject
to Swiss law, taxes, and customs.

00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:36.311
They might have to change their wall plugs.

00:03:36.311 --> 00:03:39.738
But for most nearby residents, the changes
won’t mean much.

00:03:39.738 --> 00:03:43.109
But if we think about different areas of the
world where similar processes are happening

00:03:43.109 --> 00:03:48.987
for example, the Himalaya or the Andes, these
is a completely different geopolitical situations

00:03:48.987 --> 00:03:53.250
in which also the scale of the phenomenon
is much, much bigger,

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.435
the histories are much more conflicted.

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:58.560
In the Himalayas, China and India 
disagree about their border

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.442
and have fought several times over it as it
continues to melt and shift.

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:06.942
And in the Andes, Chile and Argentina have
long disagreed about their own melting border.

00:04:06.942 --> 00:04:10.413
As climate change warms the planet 
and moves water-based borders

00:04:10.413 --> 00:04:12.495
these conflicts could worsen.

00:04:12.495 --> 00:04:16.992
Rivers, which make up over a third of the
length of all international land boundaries

00:04:16.992 --> 00:04:20.329
will be subject to extreme events 
that can change their course.

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:22.300
Coastlines will give way to rising seas

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.336
affecting exclusive economic zones, where
a country’s sovereignty 

00:04:25.336 --> 00:04:27.167
extends into the ocean.

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:30.230
And glaciers, like in the Alps, 
will continue to melt.

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:33.784
The alpine mobile border is almost like 
a laboratory

00:04:33.784 --> 00:04:38.019
a prototype of a condition that will happen
more and more

00:04:38.019 --> 00:04:40.735
in a lot of different parts of the world.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:47.690
Keep going!

